🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch
I am looking at a torch.compile() backend and with the current aot_autograd() implementation the created graphmodule treats all of the module parameters as input to the graph module. Since in my particular use case I know the parameters are frozen, I'd like to be able to precalculate subgraphs which depend on such parameters. Perhaps an additional parameter on the aot_autograd() invocation could control this behavior?
Alternatives
No response
Additional context
Sample model:
class Model(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, i, o):
        super().__init__()
        self._w = nn.Parameter(torch.randn((o, i)))
        self._b = nn.Parameter(torch.randn((o)))

    def forward(x):
        return torch.addmm(self._b, x, (self._w * self._w * self._w).t())
After compilation with aot_autograd yields:
class <lambda>(torch.nn.Module):
    def forward(self, arg0_1: f32[4096], arg1_1: f32[4096, 1024], arg2_1: f32[100, 1024]):
        mul: f32[4096, 1024] = torch.ops.aten.mul.Tensor(arg1_1, arg1_1)
        mul_1: f32[4096, 1024] = torch.ops.aten.mul.Tensor(mul, arg1_1);  mul = arg1_1 = None
        t: f32[1024, 4096] = torch.ops.aten.t.default(mul_1);  mul_1 = None
        addmm: f32[100, 4096] = torch.ops.aten.addmm.default(arg0_1, arg2_1, t);  arg0_1 = arg2_1 = t = None
        return (addmm,)

This is a rather extreme example, but the only necessary argument to the module is arg2_1.
