🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch
// torch/csrc/autograd/generated/VariableType_0.cpp
at::Tensor mul_Tensor(c10::DispatchKeySet ks, const at::Tensor & self, const at::Tensor & other) {
  // ...
  auto _tmp = ([&]() {
    at::AutoDispatchBelowADInplaceOrView guard;
    return at::redispatch::mul(ks & c10::after_autograd_keyset, self_, other_);
  })();
  auto result = std::move(_tmp);
  // ...
  return result;
}
Hello,  I notice that at::AutoDispatchBelowADInplaceOrView guard is used to add c10::autograd_dispatch_keyset_with_ADInplaceOrView to the local thread's exclude list. This set includes the following keys:
    DispatchKey::AutogradFunctionality,
    DispatchKey::AutogradOther,
    DispatchKey::AutogradNestedTensor,
    DispatchKey::ADInplaceOrView
However, the mul operation is then redispatched with a new dispatch key set, calculated as ks & c10::after_autograd_keyset.
The c10::after_autograd_keyset includes
constexpr DispatchKeySet after_autograd_keyset =
    DispatchKeySet(DispatchKeySet::FULL_AFTER, c10::DispatchKey::AutogradOther);
This process involves two steps:

Adding c10::autograd_dispatch_keyset_with_ADInplaceOrView to the exclude list.
Calculating ks & c10::after_autograd_keyset for redispatching.

It appears that for certain operations, such as multiplication, redispatching using ks & c10::after_autograd_keyset is sufficient.
I'm wondering if there's a way to optimize this into a single step. For instance, could we expand the range of the guard to avoid the need for calculating ks & c10::after_autograd_keyset? Alternatively, could we remove the guard and simply use ks & c10::after_autograd_keyset_with_new_range to redispatch the kernel?
However, I'm not sure if there are any scenarios where this could cause issues. For example, if we remove the guard and then use ks & c10::after_autograd_keyset_new_range to dispatch to kernel B, and kernel B subsequently requests another dispatch, could this result in a loop call returning to VariableType_0.cpp? If there are scenarios like this, could you please provide some insight?
Thank you for your time!
